// Class 10 Mathematics practice bank, part 1: Real Numbers to Coordinate Geometry.
StudyData.addPractice(10, "maths", {
  "real-numbers": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which of the following is an irrational number?", options: ["√49", "0.151515…", "2√3", "−5/8"], answer: 2, explain: "√49 = 7, 0.151515… is a repeating decimal (a fraction) and −5/8 is a fraction. 2√3 is a non-zero rational multiplied by the irrational √3, so it is irrational.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Euclid's division lemma states that for positive integers a and b there exist unique integers q and r such that", options: ["a = bq + r, where 0 ≤ r < b", "a = bq + r, where 0 < r ≤ b", "a = b + qr, where 0 ≤ r < b", "a = bq − r, where 0 ≤ r < q"], answer: 0, explain: "The lemma is a = bq + r with 0 ≤ r < b. The remainder is always smaller than the divisor.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The decimal expansion of 7/80 terminates after how many decimal places?", options: ["5", "4", "3", "2"], answer: 1, explain: "80 = 2⁴ × 5. Multiply top and bottom by 5³ to get 875/10⁴ = 0.0875, which has 4 decimal places (the larger of the powers 4 and 1).", level: 1 },
      { q: "The product of a non-zero rational number and an irrational number is", options: ["always an integer", "sometimes rational and sometimes irrational", "always rational", "always irrational"], answer: 3, explain: "If a rational p/q (non-zero) times an irrational x were rational, then x = (product) ÷ (p/q) would be rational, a contradiction. So the product is always irrational.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The number 7 × 11 × 13 + 13 is", options: ["a prime number", "a composite number", "a perfect square", "an irrational number"], answer: 1, explain: "7 × 11 × 13 + 13 = 13(77 + 1) = 13 × 78. It has factors other than 1 and itself, so it is composite.", level: 2 },
      { q: "For any natural number n, the number 6ⁿ ends with the digit", options: ["0", "2", "4", "6"], answer: 3, explain: "6¹ = 6, 6² = 36, 6³ = 216 … every power of 6 ends in 6. It cannot end in 0 because 6ⁿ = 2ⁿ × 3ⁿ has no prime factor 5.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which of the following rational numbers has a non-terminating repeating decimal expansion?", options: ["17/8", "13/3125", "64/455", "15/1600"], answer: 2, explain: "8 = 2³, 3125 = 5⁵ and 1600 = 2⁶ × 5² (15/1600 = 3/320, 320 = 2⁶ × 5). But 455 = 5 × 7 × 13 contains primes 7 and 13, and 64/455 is in lowest form, so it does not terminate.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The HCF of two numbers is 9 and their LCM is 180. If one of the numbers is 45, the other number is", options: ["36", "40", "90", "20"], answer: 0, explain: "HCF × LCM = product of the numbers. 9 × 180 = 1620, so the other number = 1620 ÷ 45 = 36.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): The sum of a rational number and an irrational number is irrational.\nReason (R): The sum of two irrational numbers is always irrational.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 2, explain: "A is true: if r + x were rational, then x would be rational. R is false: √2 + (−√2) = 0, which is rational.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "What is the largest number that divides 70 and 125, leaving remainders 5 and 8 respectively?", options: ["5", "13", "65", "117"], answer: 1, explain: "The number must divide 70 − 5 = 65 and 125 − 8 = 117. HCF(65, 117) = 13 (65 = 5 × 13, 117 = 9 × 13), and 13 is larger than both remainders 5 and 8, so it is valid.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The HCF of 252 and 105 is 21. If 21 = 252x + 105 × 5, then x equals", options: ["−2", "2", "−5", "5"], answer: 0, explain: "252x = 21 − 525 = −504, so x = −504 ÷ 252 = −2. (Euclid: 21 = 105 − 2 × 42 = 105 − 2(252 − 2 × 105) = 5 × 105 − 2 × 252.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): 4ⁿ can never end with the digit 0 for any natural number n.\nReason (R): The prime factorisation of 4ⁿ is 2²ⁿ, which has no factor 5.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "A number ends in 0 only if it is divisible by both 2 and 5. 4ⁿ = 2²ⁿ has no factor 5 (by the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic), so R explains A.", level: 3, keepOrder: true }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "State Euclid's division lemma.", a: "For any two positive integers a and b, there exist unique whole numbers q and r such that a = bq + r, where 0 ≤ r < b.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Without doing long division, state whether 29/343 has a terminating or a non-terminating repeating decimal expansion.", a: "343 = 7³, which is not of the form 2ᵐ5ⁿ, and 29/343 is already in lowest terms. So the decimal expansion is non-terminating and repeating.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Give an example of two irrational numbers whose product is a rational number.", a: "2√5 and √5 are both irrational, and 2√5 × √5 = 2 × 5 = 10, which is rational.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Show that 5 − √3 is an irrational number, given that √3 is irrational.", a: "Assume 5 − √3 is rational, say 5 − √3 = p/q, where p and q are integers and q ≠ 0.\nThen √3 = 5 − p/q = (5q − p)/q.\nSince 5q − p and q are integers, √3 would be rational. This contradicts the fact that √3 is irrational.\nHence 5 − √3 is irrational.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Show that every positive even integer is of the form 2q and every positive odd integer is of the form 2q + 1, where q is some integer.", a: "Take any positive integer a and b = 2. By Euclid's division lemma, a = 2q + r with r = 0 or 1.\nIf r = 0, a = 2q, which is even.\nIf r = 1, a = 2q + 1, which is odd (it leaves remainder 1 on division by 2).\nHence every even integer is 2q and every odd integer is 2q + 1.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Can 12ⁿ end with the digit 0 for any natural number n? Give a reason.", a: "A number ends in 0 only if it is divisible by 10, i.e. its prime factorisation contains both 2 and 5.\n12ⁿ = (2² × 3)ⁿ = 2²ⁿ × 3ⁿ. The prime 5 does not appear.\nBy the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic the factorisation is unique, so 12ⁿ can never end with 0.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain why 7 × 6 × 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 + 5 and 7 × 11 × 13 + 13 are composite numbers.", a: "First: 7 × 6 × 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 + 5 = 5040 + 5 = 5045 = 5(1008 + 1) = 5 × 1009. It has the factor 5 other than 1 and itself.\nSecond: 7 × 11 × 13 + 13 = 13(77 + 1) = 13 × 78, which has the factor 13 other than 1 and itself.\nA number with more than two factors is composite, so both are composite.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Prove that √5 is an irrational number.", a: "Assume √5 is rational. Then √5 = a/b, where a and b are co-prime integers and b ≠ 0.\nSquaring: 5b² = a², so 5 divides a². Hence 5 divides a. Let a = 5c.\nThen 5b² = 25c², so b² = 5c². Hence 5 divides b² and so 5 divides b.\nNow 5 is a common factor of a and b, contradicting that a and b are co-prime.\nSo our assumption is wrong. Therefore √5 is irrational.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Show that the square of any positive odd integer is of the form 8m + 1, where m is some integer.", a: "Any odd positive integer is of the form 4q + 1 or 4q + 3 (Euclid's lemma with b = 4, remainders 1 or 3 give odd numbers).\n(4q + 1)² = 16q² + 8q + 1 = 8(2q² + q) + 1 = 8m + 1, with m = 2q² + q.\n(4q + 3)² = 16q² + 24q + 9 = 8(2q² + 3q + 1) + 1 = 8m + 1, with m = 2q² + 3q + 1.\nHence the square of every odd positive integer is of the form 8m + 1.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A hall is 8 m 50 cm long and 5 m 95 cm wide. It is to be paved with identical square tiles of the largest possible size, with no cutting. Find the side of each tile and the number of tiles needed.", a: "Length = 850 cm, breadth = 595 cm. The largest tile side is HCF(850, 595).\n850 = 595 × 1 + 255\n595 = 255 × 2 + 85\n255 = 85 × 3 + 0\nHCF = 85, so the side of each tile is 85 cm.\nTiles along the length = 850 ÷ 85 = 10; along the breadth = 595 ÷ 85 = 7.\nNumber of tiles = 10 × 7 = 70.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Prove that √3 is irrational. Hence show that 2 + 5√3 is irrational.", a: "Assume √3 is rational: √3 = a/b with a, b co-prime integers, b ≠ 0.\nThen 3b² = a², so 3 divides a² and hence 3 divides a. Let a = 3c.\nThen 3b² = 9c², i.e. b² = 3c², so 3 divides b² and hence b.\nSo 3 is a common factor of a and b, contradicting that they are co-prime. Hence √3 is irrational.\nNow assume 2 + 5√3 is rational, = p/q (q ≠ 0).\nThen √3 = (p/q − 2)/5 = (p − 2q)/(5q), which is rational.\nThis contradicts the result just proved. Hence 2 + 5√3 is irrational.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Use Euclid's division algorithm to find the HCF of 867 and 255, and express it in the form 867x + 255y.", a: "867 = 255 × 3 + 102\n255 = 102 × 2 + 51\n102 = 51 × 2 + 0\nThe remainder is 0, so HCF(867, 255) = 51.\nBack-substitute:\n51 = 255 − 102 × 2\n102 = 867 − 255 × 3, so 51 = 255 − 2(867 − 255 × 3) = 255 − 2 × 867 + 6 × 255\n51 = 7 × 255 − 2 × 867.\nSo HCF = 867(−2) + 255(7), i.e. x = −2, y = 7.\nCheck: −1734 + 1785 = 51.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Prove that for every positive integer n, the number n³ − n is divisible by 6.", a: "n³ − n = n(n² − 1) = (n − 1) n (n + 1), the product of three consecutive integers.\nDivisibility by 3: by Euclid's lemma, any integer is 3q, 3q + 1 or 3q + 2. Among n − 1, n, n + 1, exactly one is of the form 3q (leaves remainder 0 when divided by 3). So the product is divisible by 3.\nDivisibility by 2: among any two consecutive integers one is even, so the product is divisible by 2.\n2 and 3 are co-prime, so the product is divisible by 2 × 3 = 6.\nHence n³ − n is divisible by 6 for every positive integer n.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A school is organising teams for Sports Day. The number of students taking part is 84 from Class 8, 126 from Class 9 and 210 from Class 10. Every team must have the same number of students, and no team can have students from different classes. The principal wants each team to be as large as possible.\n(i) Write the prime factorisation of 84.\n(ii) Find the greatest possible number of students in each team.\n(iii) How many teams will be formed in all?", a: "(i) 84 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 7 = 2² × 3 × 7.\n(ii) 126 = 2 × 3² × 7 and 210 = 2 × 3 × 5 × 7. The common factors are 2, 3 and 7, so HCF(84, 126, 210) = 2 × 3 × 7 = 42. Each team has 42 students.\n(iii) Teams = 84/42 + 126/42 + 210/42 = 2 + 3 + 5 = 10 teams.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  polynomials: {
    mcq: [
      { q: "A cubic polynomial can have at most how many zeroes?", options: ["1", "2", "3", "4"], answer: 2, explain: "A polynomial of degree n has at most n zeroes. A cubic has degree 3, so at most 3 zeroes.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The graph of a quadratic polynomial ax² + bx + c (a ≠ 0) is a", options: ["parabola", "straight line", "circle", "hyperbola"], answer: 0, explain: "The graph of a quadratic polynomial is a parabola. It opens upward if a > 0 and downward if a < 0.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The number of real zeroes of the polynomial x² + 1 is", options: ["1", "0", "2", "−1"], answer: 1, explain: "x² + 1 ≥ 1 for every real x, so it is never 0. Its graph never meets the x-axis, so it has no real zeroes.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The sum of the zeroes of the polynomial 2x² − 8x + 6 is", options: ["−4", "3", "−3", "4"], answer: 3, explain: "Sum of zeroes = −b/a = −(−8)/2 = 4. (The zeroes are 1 and 3.)", level: 1 },
      { q: "A quadratic polynomial whose zeroes have sum −3 and product 2 is", options: ["x² + 3x + 2", "x² − 3x + 2", "x² + 3x − 2", "x² − 2x + 3"], answer: 0, explain: "A polynomial is x² − (sum)x + (product) = x² − (−3)x + 2 = x² + 3x + 2.", level: 2 },
      { q: "If 3 is a zero of the polynomial x² − kx + 6, then the value of k is", options: ["−5", "5", "6", "−6"], answer: 1, explain: "Put x = 3: 9 − 3k + 6 = 0, so 3k = 15 and k = 5. (The other zero is 6/3 = 2.)", level: 2 },
      { q: "The graph of a polynomial y = p(x) cuts the x-axis at 4 different points. The degree of p(x) is", options: ["2", "3", "at least 4", "exactly 1"], answer: 2, explain: "Each point where the graph cuts the x-axis is a zero. A polynomial of degree n has at most n zeroes, so 4 distinct zeroes need degree 4 or more.", level: 2 },
      { q: "If α and β are the zeroes of 2x² − 7x + 3, then 1/α + 1/β equals", options: ["7/3", "7/2", "3/7", "−7/3"], answer: 0, explain: "α + β = 7/2 and αβ = 3/2. So 1/α + 1/β = (α + β)/(αβ) = (7/2) ÷ (3/2) = 7/3.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): The polynomial x² + 4 has no real zero.\nReason (R): The graph of y = x² + 4 does not intersect the x-axis.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "The real zeroes of a polynomial are the x-coordinates of the points where its graph meets the x-axis. y = x² + 4 stays above the x-axis (minimum value 4), so there is no real zero. R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "If α and β are the zeroes of x² − 5x + k and α − β = 1, then k equals", options: ["−6", "1", "5", "6"], answer: 3, explain: "α + β = 5 and α − β = 1 give α = 3, β = 2. So k = αβ = 6.", level: 3 },
      { q: "If the sum of the zeroes of the polynomial kx² + 2x + 3k is equal to their product, then k equals", options: ["2/3", "−3/2", "−2/3", "3/2"], answer: 2, explain: "Sum = −2/k and product = 3k/k = 3. Setting −2/k = 3 gives k = −2/3.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): The graph of y = x² − 4x + 4 cuts the x-axis at two distinct points.\nReason (R): x² − 4x + 4 = (x − 2)², so it has the single repeated zero x = 2.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 3, explain: "R is true: (x − 2)² = 0 only at x = 2. So the graph just touches the x-axis at (2, 0) and A is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "If the graph of y = p(x) does not intersect the x-axis, how many real zeroes does p(x) have?", a: "The zeroes of p(x) are the x-coordinates of the points where its graph meets the x-axis. Since there is no such point, p(x) has no real zero (0 zeroes).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "For the quadratic polynomial ax² + bx + c, write the sum and the product of its zeroes in terms of a, b and c.", a: "Sum of zeroes = −b/a and product of zeroes = c/a.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "The graph of a quadratic polynomial just touches the x-axis at exactly one point. What does this tell you about its zeroes?", a: "The polynomial has only one zero, which is repeated: its two zeroes are equal. For example, (x − 2)² touches the x-axis at x = 2.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Find a quadratic polynomial, the sum and product of whose zeroes are 3/2 and −1 respectively.", a: "A quadratic polynomial is k[x² − (sum)x + product] = k[x² − (3/2)x − 1].\nTaking k = 2, the polynomial is 2x² − 3x − 2.\nCheck: 2x² − 3x − 2 = (2x + 1)(x − 2), zeroes −1/2 and 2: sum 3/2, product −1.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Verify the relationship between the zeroes and the coefficients of 3x² − 5x − 2.", a: "3x² − 5x − 2 = 3x² − 6x + x − 2 = (x − 2)(3x + 1), so the zeroes are 2 and −1/3.\nSum = 2 − 1/3 = 5/3 and −b/a = −(−5)/3 = 5/3. ✓\nProduct = 2 × (−1/3) = −2/3 and c/a = −2/3. ✓", marks: 2 },
      { q: "If α and β are the zeroes of x² − 6x + 8, find the value of α² + β².", a: "α + β = 6 and αβ = 8.\nα² + β² = (α + β)² − 2αβ\n= 36 − 16 = 20.\n(Check: zeroes are 2 and 4, and 4 + 16 = 20.)", marks: 2 },
      { q: "One zero of the polynomial x² − 9x + k is twice the other. Find the zeroes and the value of k.", a: "Let the zeroes be α and 2α.\nSum: α + 2α = 9, so α = 3. The zeroes are 3 and 6.\nk = product of zeroes = 3 × 6 = 18.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "If α and β are the zeroes of x² − 5x + 2, form a quadratic polynomial whose zeroes are 1/α and 1/β.", a: "α + β = 5 and αβ = 2.\nSum of new zeroes = 1/α + 1/β = (α + β)/αβ = 5/2.\nProduct of new zeroes = 1/(αβ) = 1/2.\nPolynomial = k[x² − (5/2)x + 1/2]; taking k = 2 gives 2x² − 5x + 1.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "If α and β are the zeroes of x² + 8x + k such that α − β = 4, find k.", a: "α + β = −8 and α − β = 4.\nAdding: 2α = −4, so α = −2; then β = −8 − (−2) = −6.\nk = αβ = (−2)(−6) = 12.\nCheck: x² + 8x + 12 = (x + 2)(x + 6).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Find a quadratic polynomial whose zeroes are 3 + √2 and 3 − √2.", a: "Sum of zeroes = (3 + √2) + (3 − √2) = 6.\nProduct of zeroes = (3 + √2)(3 − √2) = 9 − 2 = 7.\nPolynomial = x² − (sum)x + product = x² − 6x + 7.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "If α and β are the zeroes of the polynomial 2x² − 7x + 3, find (i) α² + β², (ii) α − β (taking α > β), (iii) α²β + αβ².", a: "α + β = 7/2 and αβ = 3/2.\n(i) α² + β² = (α + β)² − 2αβ = 49/4 − 3 = 37/4.\n(ii) (α − β)² = (α + β)² − 4αβ = 49/4 − 6 = 25/4, so α − β = 5/2 (as α > β).\n(iii) α²β + αβ² = αβ(α + β) = (3/2)(7/2) = 21/4.\nCheck: the zeroes are 3 and 1/2: 9 + 1/4 = 37/4, 3 − 1/2 = 5/2, and (3)(1/2)(7/2) = 21/4. ✓", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Draw the graph of y = x² − 2x − 3 by taking x = −2, −1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4. Use it to find the zeroes of the polynomial and verify the relation between the zeroes and the coefficients.", a: "Table of values:\nx = −2: y = 4 + 4 − 3 = 5\nx = −1: y = 1 + 2 − 3 = 0\nx = 0: y = −3\nx = 1: y = 1 − 2 − 3 = −4\nx = 2: y = 4 − 4 − 3 = −3\nx = 3: y = 9 − 6 − 3 = 0\nx = 4: y = 16 − 8 − 3 = 5\nPlot the points (−2, 5), (−1, 0), (0, −3), (1, −4), (2, −3), (3, 0), (4, 5) and join them with a smooth U-shaped curve (parabola opening upward, lowest point (1, −4)).\nThe curve cuts the x-axis at x = −1 and x = 3, so the zeroes are −1 and 3.\nSum = −1 + 3 = 2 and −b/a = −(−2)/1 = 2. ✓\nProduct = (−1)(3) = −3 and c/a = −3/1 = −3. ✓", marks: 5 },
      { q: "A ball is thrown upward from the ground. Its height h (in metres) after t seconds is h = 20t − 5t². (i) Factorise the expression and find the times when h = 0. (ii) Verify the relation between the zeroes and the coefficients of −5t² + 20t. (iii) The ball is at its highest point exactly halfway between the two times found in (i). Find the maximum height.", a: "(i) h = 20t − 5t² = 5t(4 − t). h = 0 when t = 0 or t = 4. The ball leaves the ground at t = 0 s and returns at t = 4 s.\n(ii) Polynomial −5t² + 20t + 0: a = −5, b = 20, c = 0.\nSum of zeroes = 0 + 4 = 4 and −b/a = −20/(−5) = 4. ✓\nProduct = 0 × 4 = 0 and c/a = 0/(−5) = 0. ✓\n(iii) Halfway time = (0 + 4)/2 = 2 s.\nh = 20(2) − 5(2)² = 40 − 20 = 20.\nMaximum height = 20 m.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: The entrance of a road tunnel has a parabolic shape. Taking the centre of the road as the origin, the height of the tunnel roof (in metres) at a horizontal distance x metres from the centre is given by p(x) = 9 − x².\n(i) Find the zeroes of p(x). What is the width of the road at ground level?\n(ii) Find the height of the roof at the centre and at 2 m from the centre.\n(iii) Verify the relationship between the zeroes and the coefficients of p(x).", a: "(i) 9 − x² = (3 − x)(3 + x) = 0, so x = 3 or x = −3. The zeroes are 3 and −3. Width at ground level = 3 − (−3) = 6 m.\n(ii) At the centre, p(0) = 9 − 0 = 9 m. At 2 m from the centre, p(2) = 9 − 4 = 5 m.\n(iii) p(x) = −x² + 0x + 9, so a = −1, b = 0, c = 9.\nSum of zeroes = 3 + (−3) = 0 and −b/a = −0/(−1) = 0. ✓\nProduct of zeroes = 3 × (−3) = −9 and c/a = 9/(−1) = −9. ✓", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "pair-of-linear-equations-in-two-variables": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "If the graphs of two linear equations in two variables are parallel lines, the pair has", options: ["a unique solution", "no solution", "infinitely many solutions", "exactly two solutions"], answer: 1, explain: "Parallel lines never meet, so no point lies on both. The pair is inconsistent and has no solution.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The lines representing 2x + 3y = 5 and 4x + 6y = 10 are", options: ["intersecting", "parallel", "coincident", "perpendicular"], answer: 2, explain: "2/4 = 3/6 = 5/10 = 1/2. All three ratios are equal, so the lines coincide and there are infinitely many solutions.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The solution of the pair x + y = 7 and x − y = 3 is", options: ["(2, 5)", "(4, 3)", "(6, 1)", "(5, 2)"], answer: 3, explain: "Adding the equations gives 2x = 10, so x = 5. Then y = 7 − 5 = 2. Check: 5 − 2 = 3.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The graph of the linear equation x = 3 (in the x–y plane) is a line parallel to the", options: ["x-axis", "y-axis", "line y = x", "line x + y = 0"], answer: 1, explain: "Every point on x = 3 has x-coordinate 3 and any y, so it is a vertical line, parallel to the y-axis.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The pair kx + 2y = 5 and 3x + y = 1 has a unique solution if", options: ["k ≠ 6", "k = 6", "k ≠ 2", "k = 2"], answer: 0, explain: "For a unique solution a₁/a₂ ≠ b₁/b₂, i.e. k/3 ≠ 2/1, so k ≠ 6.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The pair of equations x + 2y − 4 = 0 and 2x + 4y − 12 = 0 has", options: ["a unique solution", "infinitely many solutions", "exactly two solutions", "no solution"], answer: 3, explain: "a₁/a₂ = 1/2, b₁/b₂ = 2/4 = 1/2, c₁/c₂ = −4/−12 = 1/3. Since a₁/a₂ = b₁/b₂ ≠ c₁/c₂, the lines are parallel and there is no solution.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The pair 3x + 2y = 6 and kx + 4y = 12 has infinitely many solutions for k equal to", options: ["4", "3", "6", "2"], answer: 2, explain: "We need k/3 = 4/2 = 12/6 = 2, so k = 6.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): The pair x + y = 2 and 2x + 2y = 5 has no solution.\nReason (R): For this pair a₁/a₂ = b₁/b₂ ≠ c₁/c₂.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "Writing as x + y − 2 = 0 and 2x + 2y − 5 = 0: a₁/a₂ = 1/2, b₁/b₂ = 1/2 and c₁/c₂ = 2/5. Since 1/2 = 1/2 ≠ 2/5 the lines are parallel, so there is no solution, and R is the reason.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "Assertion (A): The pair x − 2y = 0 and 3x + 4y − 20 = 0 has a unique solution.\nReason (R): The lines representing this pair are parallel.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 2, explain: "a₁/a₂ = 1/3 and b₁/b₂ = −2/4 = −1/2 are different, so the lines intersect and the solution is unique (x = 4, y = 2). The lines are not parallel, so R is false.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "Five years ago A's age was 7 times B's age. Three years from now A's age will be 3 times B's age. The present age of A is", options: ["33 years", "30 years", "28 years", "36 years"], answer: 0, explain: "a − 5 = 7(b − 5) gives a = 7b − 30. a + 3 = 3(b + 3) gives a = 3b + 6. So 7b − 30 = 3b + 6, b = 9 and a = 33. Check: 28 = 7 × 4 and 36 = 3 × 12.", level: 3 },
      { q: "If 3x + 2y = 12 and 2x + 3y = 13, then the value of x − y is", options: ["1", "5", "−1", "0"], answer: 2, explain: "Subtracting the equations: x − y = 12 − 13 = −1. (Adding gives x + y = 5, so x = 2, y = 3.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "The lines 2x + 3y = 12, the x-axis and the y-axis enclose a triangle. Its area (in square units) is", options: ["24", "12", "10", "6"], answer: 1, explain: "The line meets the x-axis at (6, 0) and the y-axis at (0, 4). Area = ½ × 6 × 4 = 12 square units.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "For what value of k does the pair 2x + 3y = 5 and 4x + 6y = k have infinitely many solutions?", a: "For infinitely many solutions a₁/a₂ = b₁/b₂ = c₁/c₂.\n2/4 = 3/6 = 5/k, so 5/k = 1/2 and k = 10.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Write the condition on the coefficients for the pair a₁x + b₁y + c₁ = 0 and a₂x + b₂y + c₂ = 0 to have a unique solution.", a: "a₁/a₂ ≠ b₁/b₂. The lines then intersect at exactly one point.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Is the pair x + y = 4 and 2x + 2y = 9 consistent? Give a reason.", a: "Write as x + y − 4 = 0 and 2x + 2y − 9 = 0.\na₁/a₂ = b₁/b₂ = 1/2 but c₁/c₂ = 4/9 ≠ 1/2, so the lines are parallel and have no common point. The pair is inconsistent.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Show that the pair 3x − 2y = 4 and 6x − 4y = 9 has no solution.", a: "Here a₁ = 3, b₁ = −2, c₁ = −4 and a₂ = 6, b₂ = −4, c₂ = −9.\na₁/a₂ = 3/6 = 1/2, b₁/b₂ = −2/−4 = 1/2, c₁/c₂ = −4/−9 = 4/9.\nSince a₁/a₂ = b₁/b₂ ≠ c₁/c₂, the lines are parallel. Hence the pair has no solution.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Find the value of k for which the pair 3x + 4y − 7 = 0 and 6x + ky − 10 = 0 has no solution.", a: "For no solution, a₁/a₂ = b₁/b₂ ≠ c₁/c₂.\n3/6 = 4/k gives k = 8.\nCheck c: c₁/c₂ = −7/−10 = 7/10 ≠ 1/2. ✓\nSo k = 8.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "If x = 2, y = −1 is the solution of the pair 3x − py = 8 and qx + 2y = 4, find p and q.", a: "Substituting x = 2, y = −1 in 3x − py = 8: 6 + p = 8, so p = 2.\nSubstituting in qx + 2y = 4: 2q − 2 = 4, so 2q = 6 and q = 3.\nHence p = 2 and q = 3.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "3 notebooks and 2 pens cost ₹ 70 together, while 1 notebook and 2 pens cost ₹ 50. Find the cost of one notebook and one pen.", a: "Let one notebook cost ₹ n and one pen ₹ p.\n3n + 2p = 70 ... (1)\nn + 2p = 50 ... (2)\nSubtract (2) from (1): 2n = 20, so n = 10.\nFrom (2): 10 + 2p = 50, so p = 20.\nA notebook costs ₹ 10 and a pen costs ₹ 20.\nCheck: 3(10) + 2(20) = 70. ✓", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The sum of the digits of a two-digit number is 9. The number obtained by reversing the digits is 27 more than the original number. Find the original number.", a: "Let the tens digit be x and the units digit y. The number is 10x + y.\nx + y = 9 ... (1)\n10y + x = (10x + y) + 27, so 9y − 9x = 27, i.e. y − x = 3 ... (2)\nAdd (1) and (2): 2y = 12, y = 6; then x = 3.\nThe number is 36.\nCheck: 63 − 36 = 27. ✓", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Solve for x and y: 1/x + 1/y = 7/12 and 1/x − 1/y = 1/12.", a: "Let 1/x = u and 1/y = v. Then u + v = 7/12 and u − v = 1/12.\nAdding: 2u = 8/12, so u = 1/3.\nSubtracting: 2v = 6/12, so v = 1/4.\nTherefore x = 1/u = 3 and y = 1/v = 4.\nCheck: 1/3 + 1/4 = 7/12 and 1/3 − 1/4 = 1/12. ✓", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A father is 4 times as old as his son. Five years ago he was 7 times as old as his son. Find their present ages.", a: "Let the son's age be s years and the father's age f years.\nf = 4s ... (1)\nf − 5 = 7(s − 5), so f = 7s − 30 ... (2)\nFrom (1) and (2): 4s = 7s − 30, so 3s = 30 and s = 10.\nf = 4 × 10 = 40.\nThe son is 10 years old and the father is 40 years old.\nCheck: five years ago they were 35 and 5, and 35 = 7 × 5. ✓", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A boat goes 24 km upstream and 36 km downstream in 6 hours. It also goes 36 km upstream and 24 km downstream in 6½ hours. Find the speed of the boat in still water and the speed of the stream.", a: "Let the speed of the boat in still water be x km/h and of the stream y km/h. Upstream speed = x − y, downstream speed = x + y.\nLet a = 1/(x − y) and b = 1/(x + y).\n24a + 36b = 6, i.e. 4a + 6b = 1 ... (1)\n36a + 24b = 13/2, i.e. 72a + 48b = 13 ... (2)\nMultiply (1) by 8: 32a + 48b = 8 ... (3)\n(2) − (3): 40a = 5, so a = 1/8 and x − y = 8.\nFrom (1): 4/8 + 6b = 1, so b = 1/12 and x + y = 12.\nAdding: 2x = 20, x = 10; then y = 2.\nThe boat's speed in still water is 10 km/h and the stream's speed is 2 km/h.\nCheck: 24/8 + 36/12 = 3 + 3 = 6 h. ✓", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Draw the graphs of 2x + y = 6 and 2x − y + 2 = 0. Find the coordinates of the vertices of the triangle formed by the two lines and the x-axis, and find its area.", a: "Line 1: 2x + y = 6. Points: (0, 6), (3, 0), (2, 2).\nLine 2: 2x − y + 2 = 0, i.e. y = 2x + 2. Points: (0, 2), (−1, 0), (1, 4).\nPlot both lines on the same axes; they cross at (1, 4). (Check: 2 + 4 = 6 and 2 − 4 + 2 = 0.)\nLine 1 meets the x-axis at (3, 0); line 2 meets it at (−1, 0).\nVertices of the triangle: (−1, 0), (3, 0) and (1, 4).\nBase = 3 − (−1) = 4 units; height = y-coordinate of the meeting point = 4 units.\nArea = ½ × 4 × 4 = 8 square units.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "A train covers a certain distance at a uniform speed. If the train were 7 km/h faster, it would take 2 hours less. If it were 7 km/h slower, it would take 3 hours more. Find the distance covered.", a: "Let the speed be v km/h and the time t hours. Distance = vt.\nFaster: (v + 7)(t − 2) = vt ⇒ vt − 2v + 7t − 14 = vt ⇒ 7t − 2v = 14 ... (1)\nSlower: (v − 7)(t + 3) = vt ⇒ vt + 3v − 7t − 21 = vt ⇒ 3v − 7t = 21 ... (2)\nAdd (1) and (2): v = 35.\nFrom (1): 7t = 14 + 70 = 84, so t = 12.\nDistance = 35 × 12 = 420 km.\nCheck: 42 × 10 = 420 and 28 × 15 = 420. ✓", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A stationery shop sells pens and notebooks. Riya buys 3 pens and 2 notebooks and pays ₹ 74. Aman buys 2 pens and 3 notebooks and pays ₹ 91.\n(i) Write the pair of linear equations for this situation, taking the price of a pen as ₹ x and of a notebook as ₹ y.\n(ii) Find the price of one pen and one notebook.\n(iii) Find the cost of 5 pens and 4 notebooks.", a: "(i) 3x + 2y = 74 and 2x + 3y = 91.\n(ii) Multiply the first by 3: 9x + 6y = 222. Multiply the second by 2: 4x + 6y = 182. Subtract: 5x = 40, so x = 8. Then 2y = 74 − 24 = 50, so y = 25. A pen costs ₹ 8 and a notebook costs ₹ 25. (Check: 2(8) + 3(25) = 91. ✓)\n(iii) 5(8) + 4(25) = 40 + 100 = ₹ 140.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "quadratic-equations": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The discriminant of the quadratic equation 2x² − 4x + 3 = 0 is", options: ["−8", "8", "16", "−16"], answer: 0, explain: "D = b² − 4ac = (−4)² − 4(2)(3) = 16 − 24 = −8.", level: 1 },
      { q: "For ax² + bx + c = 0 to be a quadratic equation, it is necessary that", options: ["b ≠ 0", "a ≠ 0", "c ≠ 0", "a = 1"], answer: 1, explain: "If a = 0 the x² term disappears and the equation becomes linear. b and c may be zero.", level: 1 },
      { q: "If the discriminant of a quadratic equation is zero, the equation has", options: ["no real roots", "two distinct real roots", "exactly three roots", "two equal real roots"], answer: 3, explain: "When b² − 4ac = 0, x = −b/(2a) twice, so the roots are real and equal.", level: 1 },
      { q: "If b² − 4ac < 0 for a quadratic equation ax² + bx + c = 0, then it has", options: ["real and equal roots", "no real roots", "two distinct real roots", "exactly one real root"], answer: 1, explain: "The square root of a negative number is not real, so the formula gives no real roots.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following is a quadratic equation?", options: ["x(x + 1) + 8 = (x + 2)(x − 2)", "x² + 3x + 1 = (x − 2)²", "(x + 2)³ = x³ − 4", "x + 1 = 2x − 5"], answer: 2, explain: "(x + 2)³ = x³ + 6x² + 12x + 8, so the equation becomes 6x² + 12x + 12 = 0 (the x³ cancels), which is quadratic. In the first two the x² terms cancel and leave linear equations (x + 12 = 0 and 7x = 3), and the fourth is linear.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The value(s) of k for which x² + kx + 9 = 0 has equal roots is/are", options: ["6", "−6", "±3", "±6"], answer: 3, explain: "D = k² − 36 = 0, so k² = 36 and k = ±6.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The product of two consecutive positive integers is 156. The smaller integer is", options: ["14", "11", "12", "13"], answer: 2, explain: "x(x + 1) = 156 ⇒ x² + x − 156 = 0 ⇒ (x + 13)(x − 12) = 0. Positive solution x = 12. The integers are 12 and 13.", level: 2 },
      { q: "If 2 is a root of x² − 3x + k = 0, the other root is", options: ["1", "2", "−1", "3"], answer: 0, explain: "Put x = 2: 4 − 6 + k = 0, so k = 2. The equation x² − 3x + 2 = (x − 1)(x − 2) has roots 1 and 2.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): The equation 2x² + 3x − 5 = 0 has no real roots.\nReason (R): If a and c have opposite signs, the discriminant of ax² + bx + c = 0 is positive.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 3, explain: "For 2x² + 3x − 5: D = 9 + 40 = 49 > 0, so it has two real roots (1 and −5/2) and A is false. R is true because ac < 0 makes −4ac positive, so b² − 4ac > 0.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "The length of a rectangular park is 3 m more than twice its breadth, and its area is 90 m². The perimeter of the park is", options: ["21 m", "42 m", "36 m", "90 m"], answer: 1, explain: "b(2b + 3) = 90 ⇒ 2b² + 3b − 90 = 0 ⇒ (b − 6)(2b + 15) = 0, b = 6 m. Length = 15 m. Perimeter = 2(6 + 15) = 42 m.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The equation (k + 1)x² − 2(k − 1)x + 1 = 0 has equal roots. The value(s) of k is/are", options: ["0 or 3", "0 only", "3 only", "−1 or 3"], answer: 0, explain: "D = 4(k − 1)² − 4(k + 1) = 0 ⇒ k² − 2k + 1 − k − 1 = 0 ⇒ k² − 3k = 0 ⇒ k = 0 or 3. Both keep k + 1 ≠ 0, so both are valid.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): If a > 0 and c < 0, the roots of ax² + bx + c = 0 are real and distinct.\nReason (R): For such an equation, b² − 4ac > 0.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "Since a > 0 and c < 0, ac < 0, so −4ac > 0 and b² − 4ac > 0 (even if b = 0). A positive discriminant gives two distinct real roots, so R explains A.", level: 3, keepOrder: true }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Find the discriminant of 3x² − 5x + 2 = 0 and state the nature of its roots.", a: "D = b² − 4ac = (−5)² − 4(3)(2) = 25 − 24 = 1.\nSince D > 0, the equation has two distinct real roots.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Write the quadratic formula for the roots of ax² + bx + c = 0 (a ≠ 0).", a: "x = [−b ± √(b² − 4ac)] / (2a), valid when b² − 4ac ≥ 0.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Is (x + 1)² = 2(x − 3) a quadratic equation? Justify.", a: "Expand: x² + 2x + 1 = 2x − 6, so x² + 7 = 0.\nIt is of the form ax² + bx + c = 0 with a = 1 ≠ 0, so it is a quadratic equation.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Find the roots of 3x² − 6x + 2 = 0 using the quadratic formula.", a: "a = 3, b = −6, c = 2.\nD = b² − 4ac = 36 − 24 = 12 > 0.\nx = (6 ± √12)/6 = (6 ± 2√3)/6 = (3 ± √3)/3.\nRoots: (3 + √3)/3 and (3 − √3)/3.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Find the value of k for which the equation kx(x − 2) + 6 = 0 has two equal roots.", a: "kx² − 2kx + 6 = 0, so a = k, b = −2k, c = 6.\nEqual roots: D = 4k² − 24k = 0 ⇒ 4k(k − 6) = 0.\nk = 0 would make the equation 6 = 0 (not quadratic), so k = 6.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Show that x = −3 is a root of 2x² + 5x − 3 = 0 and find the other root.", a: "Put x = −3: 2(9) + 5(−3) − 3 = 18 − 15 − 3 = 0. So −3 is a root.\nProduct of roots = c/a = −3/2, so the other root = (−3/2) ÷ (−3) = 1/2.\nCheck: 2(1/4) + 5(1/2) − 3 = 0.5 + 2.5 − 3 = 0. ✓", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Solve x² − 6x + 4 = 0 by the method of completing the square.", a: "x² − 6x = −4.\nAdd (6/2)² = 9 to both sides: x² − 6x + 9 = 5, i.e. (x − 3)² = 5.\nx − 3 = ±√5, so x = 3 + √5 or x = 3 − √5.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The sum of the squares of two consecutive odd positive integers is 290. Find the integers.", a: "Let the integers be x and x + 2.\nx² + (x + 2)² = 290 ⇒ 2x² + 4x + 4 = 290 ⇒ x² + 2x − 143 = 0.\nFactorise: (x + 13)(x − 11) = 0, so x = 11 (x = −13 is rejected as the integers are positive).\nThe integers are 11 and 13.\nCheck: 121 + 169 = 290. ✓", marks: 3 },
      { q: "The hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle is 20 cm. The difference between the other two sides is 4 cm. Find the lengths of the sides.", a: "Let the shorter side be x cm; the other side is (x + 4) cm.\nBy Pythagoras: x² + (x + 4)² = 20² ⇒ 2x² + 8x + 16 = 400 ⇒ x² + 4x − 192 = 0.\nD = 16 + 768 = 784 = 28², so x = (−4 ± 28)/2 = 12 or −16.\nA length cannot be negative, so x = 12.\nThe sides are 12 cm, 16 cm and 20 cm.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Find the values of k for which 2x² + kx + 3 = 0 has equal roots, and find the roots in each case.", a: "a = 2, b = k, c = 3.\nEqual roots need D = k² − 24 = 0, so k² = 24, i.e. k = 2√6 or k = −2√6.\nRepeated root x = −b/(2a) = −k/4.\nFor k = 2√6: x = −2√6/4 = −√6/2 (twice).\nFor k = −2√6: x = 2√6/4 = √6/2 (twice).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A bus covers 240 km at a uniform speed. If its speed were 20 km/h more, it would take 2 hours less for the journey. Find the original speed of the bus.", a: "Let the original speed be x km/h.\nTime taken = 240/x hours; with speed x + 20, time = 240/(x + 20) hours.\n240/x − 240/(x + 20) = 2\n240[(x + 20) − x] / [x(x + 20)] = 2 ⇒ 4800 = 2x(x + 20)\n⇒ x² + 20x − 2400 = 0.\nD = 400 + 9600 = 10000, √D = 100, so x = (−20 ± 100)/2 = 40 or −60.\nSpeed cannot be negative, so x = 40.\nThe original speed is 40 km/h.\nCheck: 240/40 = 6 h and 240/60 = 4 h, a difference of 2 h. ✓", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Two taps running together can fill a tank in 6 hours. The tap of smaller diameter alone takes 5 hours more than the tap of larger diameter alone. Find the time each tap takes to fill the tank separately.", a: "Let the larger tap alone take x hours; the smaller tap takes (x + 5) hours.\nIn one hour they fill 1/x and 1/(x + 5) of the tank; together they fill 1/6.\n1/x + 1/(x + 5) = 1/6\n⇒ (2x + 5)/[x(x + 5)] = 1/6\n⇒ 6(2x + 5) = x² + 5x\n⇒ x² − 7x − 30 = 0.\nFactorise: (x − 10)(x + 3) = 0, so x = 10 (x = −3 is rejected as time cannot be negative).\nLarger tap: 10 hours. Smaller tap: 10 + 5 = 15 hours.\nCheck: 1/10 + 1/15 = 3/30 + 2/30 = 5/30 = 1/6. ✓", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Find the values of k for which the equation (k + 4)x² + (k + 1)x + 1 = 0 has equal roots. Also find the roots for each value of k.", a: "a = k + 4, b = k + 1, c = 1 (k ≠ −4).\nEqual roots need D = (k + 1)² − 4(k + 4) = 0.\nk² + 2k + 1 − 4k − 16 = 0 ⇒ k² − 2k − 15 = 0 ⇒ (k − 5)(k + 3) = 0.\nSo k = 5 or k = −3 (both ≠ −4).\nFor k = 5: 9x² + 6x + 1 = 0 ⇒ (3x + 1)² = 0 ⇒ x = −1/3 (twice).\nFor k = −3: x² − 2x + 1 = 0 ⇒ (x − 1)² = 0 ⇒ x = 1 (twice).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Rahul's rectangular vegetable garden is 4 m longer than it is wide, and its area is 96 m². He wants to fence the garden with wire costing ₹ 50 per metre.\n(i) If the breadth is x m, form a quadratic equation in x.\n(ii) Find the length and breadth of the garden.\n(iii) Find the total cost of fencing all four sides.", a: "(i) Length = (x + 4) m. Area: x(x + 4) = 96, so x² + 4x − 96 = 0.\n(ii) Factorise: (x + 12)(x − 8) = 0, so x = 8 (x = −12 is rejected). Breadth = 8 m and length = 12 m. Check: 8 × 12 = 96. ✓\n(iii) Perimeter = 2(12 + 8) = 40 m. Cost = 40 × 50 = ₹ 2000.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "arithmetic-progressions": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which of the following lists of numbers is an AP?", options: ["1, 4, 9, 16", "2, 4, 8, 16", "3, 6, 9, 12", "1, 2, 4, 7"], answer: 2, explain: "In 3, 6, 9, 12 the difference between consecutive terms is always 3. The other lists have differences that change (3, 5, 7), (2, 4, 8) and (1, 2, 3).", level: 1 },
      { q: "The common difference of the AP 7, 3, −1, −5, … is", options: ["4", "−4", "−3", "3"], answer: 1, explain: "d = 3 − 7 = −4 (and −1 − 3 = −4, −5 − (−1) = −4).", level: 1 },
      { q: "The nth term of an AP with first term a and common difference d is", options: ["a + (n − 1)d", "a + nd", "a + (n + 1)d", "a − (n + 1)d"], answer: 0, explain: "The first term is a (no d added), the second is a + d, so the nth term is a + (n − 1)d.", level: 1 },
      { q: "If a, b and c are three consecutive terms of an AP, then", options: ["b² = ac", "2b = a + c", "a + b = 2c", "a = b + c"], answer: 1, explain: "b − a = c − b, which gives 2b = a + c: the middle term is the average of its neighbours.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The next term of the AP √2, √8, √18, √32, … is", options: ["5√3", "√36", "√64", "√50"], answer: 3, explain: "The terms are √2, 2√2, 3√2, 4√2, so d = √2 and the next term is 5√2 = √50.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which term of the AP 3, 8, 13, 18, … is 78?", options: ["15th", "17th", "14th", "16th"], answer: 3, explain: "a = 3, d = 5. 3 + (n − 1)5 = 78 ⇒ (n − 1)5 = 75 ⇒ n = 16.", level: 2 },
      { q: "In an AP, the 3rd term is 16 and the 7th term exceeds the 5th term by 12. The first term is", options: ["4", "6", "10", "−2"], answer: 0, explain: "a₇ − a₅ = 2d = 12, so d = 6. a₃ = a + 2d = 16 gives a = 16 − 12 = 4.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): The list 3, 3, 3, 3, … is an AP.\nReason (R): The common difference of an AP must be a non-zero number.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 2, explain: "Each term differs from the previous by 0, which is constant, so the list is an AP with d = 0. The common difference can be positive, negative or zero, so R is false.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "The number of multiples of 4 lying between 10 and 250 is", options: ["59", "60", "61", "58"], answer: 1, explain: "The multiples are 12, 16, …, 248. 248 = 12 + (n − 1)4 ⇒ n − 1 = 59 ⇒ n = 60.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The sum of the first n terms of an AP is Sₙ = 3n² + 2n. Its 10th term is", options: ["320", "61", "59", "11"], answer: 2, explain: "a₁₀ = S₁₀ − S₉ = (300 + 20) − (243 + 18) = 320 − 261 = 59. (Here a = 5 and d = 6, so a₁₀ = 5 + 54 = 59.)", level: 3 },
      { q: "How many terms of the AP 3, 7, 11, … must be added to get a sum of 465?", options: ["12", "14", "16", "15"], answer: 3, explain: "Sₙ = n/2[6 + 4(n − 1)] = n(2n + 1) = 465 ⇒ 2n² + n − 465 = 0 ⇒ (n − 15)(2n + 31) = 0. So n = 15 (the negative value is rejected).", level: 3 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): The 10th term from the end of the AP 4, 9, 14, …, 254 is 209.\nReason (R): The nth term from the end of a finite AP is l − (n − 1)d, where l is the last term.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "Reading the AP backwards gives an AP with first term l = 254 and difference −d = −5. The 10th term from the end is 254 − 9 × 5 = 209, so A is true and R is the reason.", level: 3, keepOrder: true }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Is the list 1, 3, 6, 10, … an AP? Give a reason.", a: "The differences are 3 − 1 = 2, 6 − 3 = 3 and 10 − 6 = 4. They are not equal, so the list is not an AP.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Write the formula for the sum of the first n terms of an AP in terms of its first term a and last term l.", a: "Sₙ = (n/2)(a + l).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Write the formula for the sum of the first n natural numbers.", a: "1 + 2 + 3 + … + n = n(n + 1)/2 (an AP with a = 1, d = 1, l = n).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Which term of the AP 40, 36, 32, … is −44? Is any term of this AP equal to 0?", a: "a = 40, d = −4.\n−44 = 40 + (n − 1)(−4) ⇒ −84 = −4(n − 1) ⇒ n − 1 = 21 ⇒ n = 22. So −44 is the 22nd term.\nFor 0: 40 − 4(n − 1) = 0 ⇒ n − 1 = 10 ⇒ n = 11. So the 11th term is 0.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The 6th term of an AP is zero. Show that its 31st term is five times its 11th term.", a: "a₆ = a + 5d = 0, so a = −5d.\na₃₁ = a + 30d = −5d + 30d = 25d.\na₁₁ = a + 10d = −5d + 10d = 5d.\nTherefore a₃₁ = 25d = 5 × 5d = 5 × a₁₁.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Find the sum of all two-digit numbers that are divisible by 7.", a: "The numbers are 14, 21, …, 98, an AP with a = 14, d = 7, l = 98.\n98 = 14 + (n − 1)7 ⇒ n − 1 = 12 ⇒ n = 13.\nS = (n/2)(a + l) = (13/2)(14 + 98) = 13 × 56 = 728.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The 4th term of an AP is 14 and the 9th term is 34. Find the AP.", a: "a + 3d = 14 ... (1)\na + 8d = 34 ... (2)\nSubtract (1) from (2): 5d = 20, so d = 4.\nFrom (1): a = 14 − 12 = 2.\nThe AP is 2, 6, 10, 14, 18, …", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The sum of three numbers in AP is 15 and their product is 105. Find the numbers.", a: "Let the numbers be a − d, a, a + d.\nSum: 3a = 15, so a = 5.\nProduct: (5 − d)(5)(5 + d) = 105 ⇒ 25 − d² = 21 ⇒ d² = 4 ⇒ d = ±2.\nFor d = 2 the numbers are 3, 5, 7 (for d = −2 they are 7, 5, 3).\nCheck: 3 + 5 + 7 = 15 and 3 × 5 × 7 = 105.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "The sum of the first n terms of an AP is 3n² + 2n. Find the AP and its 10th term.", a: "S₁ = 3 + 2 = 5, so a₁ = 5.\nS₂ = 12 + 4 = 16, so a₂ = 16 − 5 = 11.\nd = 11 − 5 = 6. The AP is 5, 11, 17, 23, …\na₁₀ = 5 + 9 × 6 = 59.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Show that the sum of the first n odd natural numbers is n². Hence find 1 + 3 + 5 + … + 49.", a: "The odd numbers 1, 3, 5, … form an AP with a = 1 and d = 2.\nSₙ = (n/2)[2(1) + (n − 1)2] = (n/2)(2n) = n².\nFor 49: 1 + 2(n − 1) = 49 ⇒ n = 25.\nSo 1 + 3 + … + 49 = 25² = 625.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A person's salary in the first year of a job is ₹ 3,00,000, and it rises by ₹ 18,000 every year. Find (i) the salary in the 12th year and (ii) the total salary received in the first 12 years.", a: "The yearly salaries form an AP with a = 3,00,000 and d = 18,000.\n(i) a₁₂ = a + 11d = 3,00,000 + 11 × 18,000 = 3,00,000 + 1,98,000 = ₹ 4,98,000.\n(ii) S₁₂ = (12/2)(a + a₁₂) = 6 × (3,00,000 + 4,98,000) = 6 × 7,98,000 = ₹ 47,88,000.\nCheck using S = (n/2)[2a + (n − 1)d] = 6[6,00,000 + 1,98,000] = 6 × 7,98,000. ✓", marks: 5 },
      { q: "The sum of the first 5 terms of an AP is 40 and the sum of its first 10 terms is 155. Find the AP and the sum of its first n terms.", a: "S₅ = (5/2)[2a + 4d] = 40 ⇒ 2a + 4d = 16 ⇒ a + 2d = 8 ... (1)\nS₁₀ = (10/2)[2a + 9d] = 155 ⇒ 2a + 9d = 31 ... (2)\nFrom (1): 2a + 4d = 16. Subtract from (2): 5d = 15, so d = 3.\nThen a = 8 − 6 = 2. The AP is 2, 5, 8, 11, …\nSₙ = (n/2)[2(2) + (n − 1)3] = (n/2)(3n + 1) = n(3n + 1)/2.\nCheck: S₅ = 5 × 16/2 = 40 and S₁₀ = 10 × 31/2 = 155. ✓", marks: 5 },
      { q: "An auditorium has rows of seats. The first row has 18 seats and each later row has 2 more seats than the row before. The last row has 60 seats. Find (i) the number of rows, (ii) the number of seats in the 15th row, (iii) the total number of seats.", a: "The seats per row form an AP with a = 18, d = 2, l = 60.\n(i) 60 = 18 + (n − 1)2 ⇒ n − 1 = 21 ⇒ n = 22. There are 22 rows.\n(ii) a₁₅ = 18 + 14 × 2 = 46 seats.\n(iii) S₂₂ = (22/2)(18 + 60) = 11 × 78 = 858 seats.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Meera starts saving money in a piggy bank. She saves ₹ 100 in the first month and then increases her saving by ₹ 40 every month, so her monthly savings form an AP.\n(i) Write the first term and the common difference of this AP.\n(ii) How much does she save in the 12th month?\n(iii) How much has she saved in total in the first 12 months?", a: "(i) First term a = 100 and common difference d = 40.\n(ii) a₁₂ = a + 11d = 100 + 11 × 40 = 100 + 440 = ₹ 540.\n(iii) S₁₂ = (12/2)(a + a₁₂) = 6 × (100 + 540) = 6 × 640 = ₹ 3840.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  triangles: {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Two figures are similar if they have", options: ["the same shape and the same size", "the same shape, not necessarily the same size", "the same size, not necessarily the same shape", "the same area only"], answer: 1, explain: "Similar figures have the same shape; one is an enlargement or reduction of the other. If the size is also the same, they are congruent.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In △ABC, D is on AB and E is on AC with DE ∥ BC. If AD = 2 cm, DB = 3 cm and AE = 4 cm, then EC is", options: ["5 cm", "7 cm", "6 cm", "8 cm"], answer: 2, explain: "By the Basic Proportionality Theorem AD/DB = AE/EC, so 2/3 = 4/EC and EC = 6 cm.", level: 1 },
      { q: "If two angles of one triangle are equal to two angles of another triangle, the triangles are similar by the criterion", options: ["AA", "SAS", "SSA", "RHS"], answer: 0, explain: "If two angles are equal, the third angles are also equal (angle sum 180°), so the triangles are similar by the AA criterion.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following sets of sides forms a right-angled triangle?", options: ["6 cm, 8 cm, 12 cm", "7 cm, 8 cm, 9 cm", "4 cm, 5 cm, 7 cm", "5 cm, 12 cm, 13 cm"], answer: 3, explain: "Only 5² + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169 = 13² satisfies the converse of the Pythagoras theorem.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The areas of two similar triangles are 36 cm² and 100 cm². The ratio of their corresponding sides is", options: ["5 : 3", "9 : 25", "3 : 5", "25 : 9"], answer: 2, explain: "The ratio of areas equals the square of the ratio of corresponding sides, so the side ratio = √(36/100) = 6/10 = 3 : 5 (taking the triangles in the order given).", level: 2 },
      { q: "△ABC ~ △DEF, AB = 6 cm and DE = 9 cm. If the perimeter of △ABC is 20 cm, the perimeter of △DEF is", options: ["30 cm", "40/3 cm", "45 cm", "25 cm"], answer: 0, explain: "The ratio of perimeters equals the ratio of corresponding sides: 20/P = 6/9, so P = 20 × 9/6 = 30 cm.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A 10 m long ladder reaches a window 8 m above the ground. The distance of the foot of the ladder from the wall is", options: ["2 m", "4 m", "6 m", "√164 m"], answer: 2, explain: "By Pythagoras, distance² = 10² − 8² = 100 − 64 = 36, so the distance is 6 m.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): The triangles with sides 3 cm, 4 cm, 5 cm and 6 cm, 8 cm, 10 cm are similar.\nReason (R): Two triangles are similar if their corresponding sides are in the same ratio.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "3/6 = 4/8 = 5/10 = 1/2, so the sides are proportional, and the SSS similarity criterion (R) gives A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "In △ABC, DE ∥ BC where D lies on AB and E on AC. If AD = x, DB = x − 2, AE = x + 2 and EC = x − 1, then x equals", options: ["3", "4", "5", "6"], answer: 1, explain: "AD/DB = AE/EC ⇒ x/(x − 2) = (x + 2)/(x − 1) ⇒ x(x − 1) = (x + 2)(x − 2) ⇒ x² − x = x² − 4 ⇒ x = 4.", level: 2 },
      { q: "In △ABC, ∠B = 90° and BD ⟂ AC. If AD = 4 cm and DC = 9 cm, then BD is", options: ["6 cm", "36 cm", "13 cm", "5 cm"], answer: 0, explain: "△ADB ~ △BDC, so BD² = AD × DC = 4 × 9 = 36 and BD = 6 cm.", level: 3 },
      { q: "In trapezium ABCD, AB ∥ DC and AB = 2 CD. The diagonals meet at O. If the area of △AOB is 84 cm², the area of △COD is", options: ["168 cm²", "42 cm²", "21 cm²", "10.5 cm²"], answer: 2, explain: "△AOB ~ △COD (AA), so area ratio = (AB/CD)² = 4. Area of △COD = 84 ÷ 4 = 21 cm².", level: 3 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): A triangle with sides 8 cm, 15 cm and 16 cm is right-angled.\nReason (R): In a right-angled triangle, the square of the hypotenuse equals the sum of the squares of the other two sides.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 3, explain: "8² + 15² = 64 + 225 = 289 = 17², but 16² = 256. The longest side squared is not equal to the sum, so A is false. R is the true Pythagoras theorem.", level: 3, keepOrder: true }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "State the Basic Proportionality Theorem (Thales theorem).", a: "If a line is drawn parallel to one side of a triangle to intersect the other two sides at distinct points, then it divides the two sides in the same ratio.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State the AA criterion of similarity of triangles.", a: "If two angles of one triangle are respectively equal to two angles of another triangle, then the two triangles are similar.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "The sides of a triangle are 7 cm, 24 cm and 25 cm. Is it a right-angled triangle? Give a reason.", a: "7² + 24² = 49 + 576 = 625 = 25².\nBy the converse of the Pythagoras theorem, the triangle is right-angled, with the right angle opposite the side of 25 cm.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "In △ABC, D and E are points on AB and AC with AD = 3 cm, DB = 4.5 cm, AE = 4 cm and EC = 6 cm. Is DE ∥ BC? Give a reason.", a: "AD/DB = 3/4.5 = 2/3.\nAE/EC = 4/6 = 2/3.\nSince AD/DB = AE/EC, by the converse of the Basic Proportionality Theorem, DE ∥ BC.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "△ABC ~ △PQR and their areas are 81 cm² and 49 cm². If the altitude AD of △ABC is 4.5 cm, find the corresponding altitude PS of △PQR.", a: "The ratio of areas of similar triangles equals the square of the ratio of their corresponding altitudes.\n81/49 = (AD/PS)² ⇒ AD/PS = 9/7.\nPS = AD × 7/9 = 4.5 × 7/9 = 3.5 cm.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A pole 6 m high casts a shadow 4 m long on the ground. At the same time a tower casts a shadow 28 m long. Find the height of the tower.", a: "At the same time the sun's rays make the same angle with the ground, and pole and tower are both vertical. By the AA criterion the two right triangles (object, shadow, ray) are similar.\nHeight of tower / 28 = 6 / 4\nHeight = 28 × 6/4 = 42 m.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Prove that the altitude of an equilateral triangle of side 2a is a√3.", a: "In equilateral △ABC with side 2a, draw AD ⟂ BC. D is the midpoint of BC, so BD = a.\nIn right △ABD: AD² = AB² − BD² = (2a)² − a² = 4a² − a² = 3a².\nHence AD = a√3.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "In △ABC, ∠B = 90° and BD ⟂ AC. Prove that BD² = AD × DC.", a: "In △ABC, ∠ABD + ∠DBC = 90° (since ∠B = 90°).\nIn right △BDC, ∠DBC + ∠C = 90°. So ∠ABD = ∠C.\nIn △ADB and △BDC: ∠ADB = ∠BDC = 90° and ∠ABD = ∠C.\nBy AA, △ADB ~ △BDC.\nTherefore AD/BD = BD/DC, which gives BD² = AD × DC.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "ABCD is a trapezium with AB ∥ DC. Its diagonals AC and BD intersect at O. Prove that OA/OC = OB/OD.", a: "In △AOB and △COD:\n∠OAB = ∠OCD (alternate angles, AB ∥ DC with transversal AC).\n∠OBA = ∠ODC (alternate angles, transversal BD).\n(Also ∠AOB = ∠COD, vertically opposite angles.)\nBy AA, △AOB ~ △COD.\nCorresponding sides are proportional: OA/OC = OB/OD = AB/CD.\nHence OA/OC = OB/OD.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "The perimeters of two similar triangles are 24 cm and 36 cm. If the area of the smaller triangle is 40 cm², find the area of the larger triangle.", a: "For similar triangles the ratio of perimeters equals the ratio of corresponding sides: 24/36 = 2/3.\nThe ratio of areas = (2/3)² = 4/9.\n40 / (area of larger) = 4/9\nArea of larger = 40 × 9/4 = 90 cm².", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Prove that if a line is drawn parallel to one side of a triangle to intersect the other two sides at distinct points, it divides the two sides in the same ratio.", a: "Given: In △ABC, DE ∥ BC, with D on AB and E on AC. To prove: AD/DB = AE/EC.\nConstruction: Join BE and CD. Draw EN ⟂ AB and DM ⟂ AC.\nProof: ar(△ADE) = ½ × AD × EN and ar(△DBE) = ½ × DB × EN.\nSo ar(△ADE)/ar(△DBE) = AD/DB ... (1)\nSimilarly ar(△ADE)/ar(△DEC) = (½ × AE × DM)/(½ × EC × DM) = AE/EC ... (2)\n△DBE and △DEC lie on the same base DE and between the same parallels DE and BC, so ar(△DBE) = ar(△DEC) ... (3)\nFrom (1), (2) and (3): AD/DB = AE/EC. Hence proved.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Prove that in a right-angled triangle, the square of the hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the squares of the other two sides.", a: "Given: right △ABC with ∠B = 90°. To prove: AC² = AB² + BC².\nConstruction: Draw BD ⟂ AC.\nIn △ADB and △ABC: ∠A is common and ∠ADB = ∠ABC = 90°. By AA, △ADB ~ △ABC.\nSo AD/AB = AB/AC, giving AB² = AD × AC ... (1)\nIn △BDC and △ABC: ∠C is common and ∠BDC = ∠ABC = 90°. By AA, △BDC ~ △ABC.\nSo CD/BC = BC/AC, giving BC² = CD × AC ... (2)\nAdd (1) and (2): AB² + BC² = AC(AD + CD) = AC × AC = AC².\nHence AC² = AB² + BC².", marks: 5 },
      { q: "In △ABC, D is a point on AB and E a point on AC such that DE ∥ BC and AD/DB = 3/5. (i) Prove that △ADE ~ △ABC. (ii) Find the ratio of the areas of △ADE and △ABC. (iii) If the area of △ABC is 128 cm², find the area of △ADE and of the trapezium DBCE.", a: "(i) In △ADE and △ABC: ∠A is common and ∠ADE = ∠ABC (corresponding angles, DE ∥ BC). By AA, △ADE ~ △ABC.\n(ii) AD/DB = 3/5, so AD/AB = 3/(3 + 5) = 3/8.\nRatio of areas = (AD/AB)² = 9/64.\n(iii) Area of △ADE = 128 × 9/64 = 18 cm².\nArea of trapezium DBCE = 128 − 18 = 110 cm².\n(Ratio of areas of △ADE to trapezium DBCE = 18 : 110 = 9 : 55.)", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A girl 1.5 m tall stands in the sun and her shadow is 2 m long. At the same time a tree nearby casts a shadow 12 m long. Assume that the girl and the tree both stand vertically.\n(i) Which criterion shows that the triangle formed by the girl and her shadow is similar to the triangle formed by the tree and its shadow?\n(ii) Find the height of the tree.\n(iii) Find the distance from the top of the tree to the tip of its shadow.", a: "(i) Both objects are vertical, so each makes a 90° angle with the ground. The sun's rays are parallel, so they make the same angle with the ground. Two angles are equal, so the triangles are similar by the AA criterion.\n(ii) Height of tree / 12 = 1.5 / 2 ⇒ height = 12 × 1.5/2 = 9 m.\n(iii) Distance = √(9² + 12²) = √(81 + 144) = √225 = 15 m (by Pythagoras theorem).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "coordinate-geometry": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The distance of the point (−5, 12) from the origin is", options: ["7", "17", "13", "12"], answer: 2, explain: "Distance = √((−5)² + 12²) = √(25 + 144) = √169 = 13.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The midpoint of the line segment joining (2, −4) and (6, 8) is", options: ["(4, 2)", "(8, 4)", "(−4, −2)", "(2, 4)"], answer: 0, explain: "Midpoint = ((2 + 6)/2, (−4 + 8)/2) = (4, 2).", level: 1 },
      { q: "The coordinates of any point on the y-axis are of the form", options: ["(x, 0)", "(0, y)", "(x, x)", "(y, y)"], answer: 1, explain: "Every point on the y-axis has x-coordinate 0, so it is (0, y).", level: 1 },
      { q: "If P divides the segment joining (x₁, y₁) and (x₂, y₂) internally in the ratio m : n, the x-coordinate of P is", options: ["(mx₂ + nx₁)/(m + n)", "(mx₁ + nx₂)/(m + n)", "(x₁ + x₂)/(m + n)", "(mx₂ − nx₁)/(m + n)"], answer: 0, explain: "The section formula gives x = (mx₂ + nx₁)/(m + n): the second point's coordinate is multiplied by m, the first by n.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The point on the x-axis which is equidistant from (2, −5) and (−2, 9) is", options: ["(7, 0)", "(0, −7)", "(−7, 0)", "(−5, 0)"], answer: 2, explain: "Let the point be (x, 0). Then (x − 2)² + 25 = (x + 2)² + 81 ⇒ −4x + 29 = 4x + 85 ⇒ −8x = 56 ⇒ x = −7.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The x-axis divides the line segment joining (2, −3) and (5, 6) in the ratio", options: ["2 : 1", "1 : 2", "1 : 3", "3 : 1"], answer: 1, explain: "Let the ratio be k : 1. The y-coordinate of the dividing point is (6k − 3)/(k + 1) = 0, so k = 1/2, i.e. the ratio is 1 : 2.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The ends of a diameter of a circle are (−3, 5) and (7, −1). The centre of the circle is", options: ["(2, 2)", "(4, 4)", "(−5, 3)", "(5, −3)"], answer: 0, explain: "The centre is the midpoint of the diameter: ((−3 + 7)/2, (5 − 1)/2) = (2, 2).", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): The points (2, 5), (4, 7) and (6, 9) are collinear.\nReason (R): Three points A, B, C are collinear if the sum of two of the distances AB, BC, CA equals the third.", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "AB = √(4 + 4) = 2√2, BC = 2√2 and AC = √(16 + 16) = 4√2. Since AB + BC = AC, the points are collinear, as R states.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "The triangle with vertices (0, 0), (6, 0) and (3, 3√3) is", options: ["scalene", "isosceles but not equilateral", "equilateral", "right-angled"], answer: 2, explain: "Side 1 = 6. Distance from (0, 0) to (3, 3√3) = √(9 + 27) = 6. Distance from (6, 0) to (3, 3√3) = √(9 + 27) = 6. All sides are equal, so it is equilateral.", level: 2 },
      { q: "If the point (−3, k) divides the line segment joining A(−5, −4) and B(−2, 5) in the ratio 2 : 1, then k equals", options: ["4", "1", "−2", "2"], answer: 3, explain: "y = (2 × 5 + 1 × (−4))/(2 + 1) = 6/3 = 2. (Check x: (2 × (−2) + (−5))/3 = −3. ✓)", level: 3 },
      { q: "Three vertices of a parallelogram ABCD, taken in order, are A(1, 2), B(4, 3) and C(6, 6). The fourth vertex D is", options: ["(−1, −1)", "(9, 7)", "(3, 5)", "(5, 3)"], answer: 2, explain: "The diagonals bisect each other, so midpoint of AC = midpoint of BD. Midpoint of AC = (3.5, 4). D = (2 × 3.5 − 4, 2 × 4 − 3) = (3, 5). Check: AB = (3, 1) and DC = (3, 1).", level: 3 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): A point (x, y) equidistant from (3, 6) and (−3, 4) satisfies 2x + y − 5 = 0.\nReason (R): A point P is equidistant from A and B when PA = PB, which is the same as PA² = PB².", options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 3, explain: "R is true. Using it: (x − 3)² + (y − 6)² = (x + 3)² + (y − 4)² ⇒ −6x − 12y + 45 = 6x − 8y + 25 ⇒ 12x + 4y − 20 = 0 ⇒ 3x + y − 5 = 0, not 2x + y − 5 = 0. So A is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Write the distance formula for the distance between P(x₁, y₁) and Q(x₂, y₂).", a: "PQ = √[(x₂ − x₁)² + (y₂ − y₁)²].", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Find the coordinates of the points on the y-axis which are at a distance of 5 units from the origin.", a: "A point on the y-axis is (0, y). Its distance from the origin is |y| = 5, so y = 5 or y = −5.\nThe points are (0, 5) and (0, −5).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Find the midpoint of the line segment joining (−2, 6) and (4, −10).", a: "Midpoint = ((−2 + 4)/2, (6 + (−10))/2) = (1, −2).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Find the values of y for which the distance between the points P(2, −3) and Q(10, y) is 10 units.", a: "PQ = 10 ⇒ (10 − 2)² + (y + 3)² = 100\n64 + (y + 3)² = 100 ⇒ (y + 3)² = 36 ⇒ y + 3 = ±6.\nSo y = 3 or y = −9.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "In what ratio does the y-axis divide the line segment joining (−4, 5) and (6, −3)? Also find the point of division.", a: "Let the y-axis divide the segment in the ratio k : 1. The point is ((6k − 4)/(k + 1), (−3k + 5)/(k + 1)).\nOn the y-axis the x-coordinate is 0: 6k − 4 = 0, so k = 2/3. The ratio is 2 : 3.\ny = (−3 × 2 + 5 × 3)/(2 + 3) = 9/5.\nThe point of division is (0, 9/5).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The midpoint of the line segment PQ is (1, −2). If P is (−3, 4), find the coordinates of Q.", a: "Let Q = (x, y). Midpoint: (−3 + x)/2 = 1 ⇒ x = 5.\n(4 + y)/2 = −2 ⇒ y = −8.\nSo Q is (5, −8).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The ends of a diameter of a circle are (−2, 5) and (6, −1). Find the centre and the radius of the circle.", a: "Centre = midpoint of the diameter = ((−2 + 6)/2, (5 − 1)/2) = (2, 2).\nLength of diameter = √[(6 + 2)² + (−1 − 5)²] = √(64 + 36) = 10.\nRadius = 10 ÷ 2 = 5 units.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Show that the points A(1, 2), B(4, 6) and C(8, 3) are the vertices of an isosceles right-angled triangle.", a: "AB = √[(4 − 1)² + (6 − 2)²] = √(9 + 16) = 5.\nBC = √[(8 − 4)² + (3 − 6)²] = √(16 + 9) = 5.\nAC = √[(8 − 1)² + (3 − 2)²] = √(49 + 1) = √50.\nAB = BC, so the triangle is isosceles.\nAB² + BC² = 25 + 25 = 50 = AC², so by the converse of Pythagoras the angle at B is 90°.\nHence ABC is an isosceles right-angled triangle.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Find the coordinates of the points of trisection of the line segment joining A(−3, 2) and B(6, −4).", a: "Let P and Q be the points of trisection, so AP : PB = 1 : 2 and AQ : QB = 2 : 1.\nP = ((1 × 6 + 2 × (−3))/3, (1 × (−4) + 2 × 2)/3) = (0/3, 0/3) = (0, 0).\nQ = ((2 × 6 + 1 × (−3))/3, (2 × (−4) + 1 × 2)/3) = (9/3, −6/3) = (3, −2).\nThe points of trisection are (0, 0) and (3, −2).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Show that the points A(−1, −1), B(2, 5) and C(5, 11) are collinear.", a: "AB = √[(2 + 1)² + (5 + 1)²] = √(9 + 36) = √45 = 3√5.\nBC = √[(5 − 2)² + (11 − 5)²] = √(9 + 36) = 3√5.\nAC = √[(5 + 1)² + (11 + 1)²] = √(36 + 144) = √180 = 6√5.\nAB + BC = 3√5 + 3√5 = 6√5 = AC.\nSince the sum of two distances equals the third, the three points lie on a straight line. B is the midpoint of AC.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "The points A(1, −2), B(4, a), C(b, 6) and D(0, 3) are the vertices of a parallelogram ABCD taken in order. Find a and b. Also find the lengths of the diagonals AC and BD.", a: "The diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other, so the midpoint of AC = the midpoint of BD.\nMidpoint of AC = ((1 + b)/2, (−2 + 6)/2) = ((1 + b)/2, 2).\nMidpoint of BD = ((4 + 0)/2, (a + 3)/2) = (2, (a + 3)/2).\nEquate x: (1 + b)/2 = 2 ⇒ b = 3.\nEquate y: 2 = (a + 3)/2 ⇒ a = 1.\nSo A(1, −2), B(4, 1), C(3, 6), D(0, 3).\nAC = √[(3 − 1)² + (6 + 2)²] = √(4 + 64) = √68 = 2√17.\nBD = √[(0 − 4)² + (3 − 1)²] = √(16 + 4) = √20 = 2√5.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Find the ratio in which the line 2x + y − 7 = 0 divides the line segment joining A(0, 2) and B(6, −4). Also find the coordinates of the point of division.", a: "Let the line divide AB at P in the ratio k : 1.\nP = ((k × 6 + 1 × 0)/(k + 1), (k × (−4) + 1 × 2)/(k + 1)) = (6k/(k + 1), (2 − 4k)/(k + 1)).\nP lies on 2x + y − 7 = 0:\n12k/(k + 1) + (2 − 4k)/(k + 1) − 7 = 0\n12k + 2 − 4k − 7(k + 1) = 0 ⇒ 8k + 2 − 7k − 7 = 0 ⇒ k − 5 = 0 ⇒ k = 5.\nThe ratio is 5 : 1.\nP = (30/6, (2 − 20)/6) = (5, −3).\nCheck: 2(5) + (−3) − 7 = 0. ✓", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Find the coordinates of the point equidistant from the vertices A(1, 1), B(5, 1) and C(3, 5) of a triangle (its circumcentre), and find the radius of the circle passing through A, B and C.", a: "Let P(x, y) be equidistant from A, B and C.\nPA² = PB²: (x − 1)² + (y − 1)² = (x − 5)² + (y − 1)² ⇒ (x − 1)² = (x − 5)² ⇒ −2x + 1 = −10x + 25 ⇒ 8x = 24 ⇒ x = 3.\nPA² = PC²: (3 − 1)² + (y − 1)² = (3 − 3)² + (y − 5)² ⇒ 4 + y² − 2y + 1 = y² − 10y + 25 ⇒ 8y = 20 ⇒ y = 5/2.\nThe point is P(3, 5/2).\nRadius = PC = |5 − 5/2| = 5/2 units.\nCheck: PA = √(4 + 9/4) = √(25/4) = 5/2. ✓", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: On a town map drawn on a grid (1 unit = 1 km), Ravi's house H is at (2, 3), his school S is at (6, 6) and a park P is at (10, 9).\n(i) Find the distance between Ravi's house and the school.\n(ii) Find the coordinates of the midpoint of HS.\n(iii) Show that H, S and P lie on a straight line.", a: "(i) HS = √[(6 − 2)² + (6 − 3)²] = √(16 + 9) = 5 km.\n(ii) Midpoint of HS = ((2 + 6)/2, (3 + 6)/2) = (4, 4.5).\n(iii) SP = √[(10 − 6)² + (9 − 6)²] = √(16 + 9) = 5 km.\nHP = √[(10 − 2)² + (9 − 3)²] = √(64 + 36) = 10 km.\nHS + SP = 5 + 5 = 10 = HP, so H, S and P are collinear (S is the midpoint of HP).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
